      correct: 0,
      explanation: 'When galaxies move away from Earth, their light is stretched to longer (redder) wavelengths — a Doppler redshift. The observation that distant galaxies are redshifted supports the conclusion that the universe is expanding, consistent with the Big Bang theory.',
      diveDeep: 'The Doppler effect applies to light as well as sound: objects moving away produce redshift (longer wavelengths); objects moving toward the observer produce blueshift (shorter wavelengths). Edwin Hubble discovered in 1929 that nearly all galaxies are redshifted and that more distant galaxies have greater redshifts (Hubble\'s Law), indicating they are receding faster. This is the primary observational evidence for the expanding universe and the Big Bang. Students should connect redshift to the Big Bang (along with cosmic microwave background radiation and the abundance of hydrogen and helium). The universe is approximately 13.8 billion years old.',
