      explanation: 'Gradient = change in elevation ÷ horizontal distance = (180 m − 60 m) ÷ 6 km = 120 m ÷ 6 km = 20 m/km.',
      diveDeep: 'Topographic map gradient calculations are a standard Regents skill. Always: (1) identify the elevations at both points, (2) calculate the difference, (3) measure or read the horizontal distance, (4) divide. Units must match — if distance is in km and elevation in meters, the gradient is in m/km. Closely spaced contour lines = steep gradient; widely spaced lines = gentle gradient. On the actual exam, horizontal distance is often given or can be measured using the map scale. Students frequently make errors by forgetting to subtract the smaller from the larger elevation, or by using the wrong units.',
