      explanation: 'As stream velocity decreases, the largest and heaviest particles (boulders) are deposited first because they require the highest velocity to remain in suspension or be transported as bedload.',
      diveDeep: 'The Hjulström curve (stream competence graph) in the Reference Tables shows the relationship between stream velocity and particle transport. Larger particles require higher velocities to be eroded and transported; they are deposited first as velocity drops. The order of deposition as velocity decreases: boulders → cobbles → pebbles/gravel → sand → silt → clay. Interestingly, clay requires higher velocities to erode than silt (due to cohesion) but settles very slowly once suspended. Students should be able to read the graph to determine whether a particle of given size is being eroded, transported, or deposited at a given velocity.',
