      diveDeep: 'The rock cycle is one of the fundamental concepts in Earth science. The key distinction for metamorphism: high heat and pressure change the mineralogy and texture of existing rock WITHOUT melting it. Common metamorphic transformations: shale → slate → phyllite → schist → gneiss (with increasing grade); limestone → marble; sandstone → quartzite. If the rock does melt, it becomes magma, and upon cooling forms igneous rock. Students should be able to trace any path through the rock cycle and identify the process for each transition. The Reference Tables include a rock cycle diagram.',
