      diveDeep: 'Isobars are lines connecting points of equal atmospheric pressure on a weather map, measured in millibars (mb). The pressure gradient force is what initiates wind motion, directed from high to low pressure. The Coriolis effect then deflects the resulting winds. Widely spaced isobars = gentle gradient = light winds; closely spaced isobars = steep gradient = strong winds. Around low-pressure centers (storms), isobars are typically closely packed and form concentric circles. Students should be able to estimate wind direction (counterclockwise around lows in the Northern Hemisphere) and strength (from isobar spacing) from a weather map.',
