      diveDeep: 'Plate tectonic boundary types and their features: (1) Divergent boundaries: plates move apart; mid-ocean ridges form; new oceanic crust created (seafloor spreading); shallow earthquakes; example: Mid-Atlantic Ridge. (2) Convergent boundaries: plates collide; if oceanic-continental → subduction trench + volcanic arc + deep earthquakes; if oceanic-oceanic → island arc; if continental-continental → mountain range (no subduction, no volcanoes); example: Andes, Himalayas. (3) Transform boundaries: plates slide horizontally; no crust created or destroyed; strike-slip faults; example: San Andreas Fault. Harry Hess\'s seafloor spreading hypothesis (1960) was crucial evidence for plate tectonics. The youngest seafloor is at mid-ocean ridges; oldest is near subduction zones.',
