      diveDeep: 'The Moon\'s sidereal period (one orbit relative to distant stars) is 27.3 days, but the synodic period (one complete phase cycle, relative to the Sun) is 29.5 days because Earth moves along its orbit. Moon phases result from the changing angle between the Moon, Earth, and Sun as seen from Earth\'s surface. New moon → first quarter → full moon → third quarter → new moon. The illuminated portion we see changes because only half the Moon is lit by the Sun at any time. Students should know that the same side of the Moon always faces Earth (synchronous rotation) and be able to identify phases from diagrams. Tides are also linked to lunar phases.',
