      diveDeep: 'The angle of insolation is the fundamental control on latitudinal temperature differences. A simple demonstration: shine a flashlight straight down (perpendicular) → small bright circle; tilt the flashlight → larger, dimmer ellipse. The same amount of energy is spread over more area at lower angles. Earth\'s axial tilt (23.5°) means this angle changes seasonally at any given latitude. At the summer solstice, the sun is directly overhead at the Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N), providing maximum insolation to that latitude. Students should connect angle of insolation to seasons, climate zones, and the reason why polar regions are cold. Also: longer days at high latitudes in summer partially compensate for the lower sun angle.',
