      image: '/images/exams/es-january-2024/context_48_50.png',
      explanation: 'Solar energy is the primary driver of the water cycle. The Sun provides the heat energy required to evaporate water from oceans, lakes, and land surfaces, converting liquid water to water vapor that rises into the atmosphere.',
      diveDeep: 'The water cycle (hydrologic cycle) involves continuous movement of water through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff, and infiltration. Solar energy drives evaporation — the conversion of liquid water to water vapor — which is the crucial first step. Gravity drives precipitation (falling water) and surface runoff. Transpiration (water vapor released by plants) is also driven by solar energy. The water cycle distributes fresh water globally and is essential for all life. Students should know that most evaporation (about 85%) occurs from the oceans. On the Regents, questions may ask about the energy source (Sun), the process that returns water to the atmosphere (evaporation/transpiration), or how human activities affect the cycle (deforestation → less transpiration; urbanization → more runoff, less infiltration).',
